---
source: crates/crossfade-cli/tests/cli.rs
expression: text(&out.stdout)
---
Convert a media file

Usage: crossfade convert [OPTIONS] --output <OUTPUT> <INPUT>

Arguments:
  <INPUT>  File to read: wav, flac, aiff/aifc, ogg (FLAC or Vorbis), mp3, mp4/m4a (ALAC; AAC in a build with the aac feature) or .aac (ADTS, same build)

Options:
  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          File to write; its extension picks the container: .wav, .flac, .aiff/.aif/.aifc, .oga/.ogg (Ogg FLAC)
      --sample-format <SAMPLE_FORMAT>
          Output sample format; defaults to the input's [possible values: u8, s16, s24, s32, f32, f64]
      --sample-rate <HZ>
          Output sample rate, such as 48000 or 44.1kHz; defaults to the input's. Converted after the filters
      --channels <LAYOUT>
          Output channels: a layout (mono, stereo, 5.1, ...) or a count; defaults to the input's. Converted after the filters
      --codec <CODEC>
          How audio is written: copy the packets unchanged, raw pcm, or flac. Defaults to the container's own [possible values: copy, pcm, flac]
      --compression-level <COMPRESSION_LEVEL>
          FLAC effort from 0 to 8: 0 is fastest, 8 gives the smallest file
      --threads <THREADS>
          Threads for coding: auto (the default) uses the machine's cores, 1 keeps everything on one thread. The output is the same either way [default: auto]
      --force
          Replace an existing output file
      --emit-job
          Print the job as JSON and exit without touching any file
  -h, --help
          Print help

Processing:
      --filter <FILTER>   A filter: a name, an optional value, then key=value words, such as "gain -3dB" or "fade out=3s curve=equal-power". Repeat for more; they run in the order given
      --gain <dB>         Change the level, such as -6dB (not with --normalize, which sets the level itself)
      --start <TIME>      Start at this time, such as 10s or 1:30
      --end <TIME>        End at this time
      --duration <TIME>   Keep this long from the start
      --fade-in <TIME>    Fade in from silence over this long
      --fade-out <TIME>   Fade out to silence over this long, ending at the end (at most 10 minutes)
      --normalize <LUFS>  Bring the integrated loudness to this target, such as -16LUFS (measures the whole programme first)
      --true-peak <dBTP>  The true peak --normalize may not pass [default: -1dBTP]
      --dither <DITHER>   How processed audio is rounded to an integer format: tpdf (triangular dither, the default) or none. Only used when something is processed; float output is never dithered [possible values: tpdf, none]

Processing order: --start/--end/--duration (one trim), then each --filter in the order given, then --fade-in/--fade-out (one fade), --gain, --normalize; last, the conversions --sample-rate and --channels ask for. The job lists the filters in that order.

Filters: gain <dB> | trim start=<time> end=<time> duration=<time> | fade in=<time> out=<time> curve=linear|equal-power|s-curve | normalize <LUFS> peak=<dBTP> | resample <rate> quality=fast|good|best | mix <layout or count> matrix=<rows>
Units are written: 10s, 500ms, 1:30, 1:02:03.5; -3dB; -16LUFS; -1dBTP; 48000, 48000Hz, 44.1kHz.
Layouts: mono, stereo, 2.1, 3.0, 3.1, 4.0, quad, quad-side, 5.0, 5.0-back, 5.1, 5.1-back, 6.1, 7.1.
